# Two separate peer conflicts make a default `npm ci` fail outright — which
# means a Vercel build fails, not just a noisy local install. Both come from
# adding @better-auth/oauth-provider (the client plugin the login page needs to
# resume an interrupted OAuth authorization):
#
#   1. The one npm reports first, and a REQUIRED peer:
#      @better-auth/oauth-provider@1.7.3 wants better-auth@^1.7.3, and this app
#      pins better-auth@1.7.2. We accept that skew deliberately — it is the same
#      combination the API already runs, and the frontend loads only this
#      package's client.mjs.
#   2. An OPTIONAL peer that npm still escalates to a hard error:
#      better-call@1.4.0 wants zod@^4, and this app is on zod@^3.24.1. Bumping
#      better-auth to 1.7.3 does not clear this one.
#
# Verified with a clean `npm ci` from a copy of package.json + package-lock.json
# + this file: resolves better-auth 1.7.2, @better-auth/oauth-provider 1.7.3,
# zod 3.25.76 — no runtime change. An audit of all 324 installed packages found
# no required peer unmet other than the deliberate 1.7.2/1.7.3 skew above, and
# no unsatisfied regular dependency.
#
# Cost of this flag: npm no longer auto-installs peer dependencies, and it will
# not warn about genuine peer conflicts here. @testing-library/dom is declared
# explicitly in devDependencies for exactly that reason — it used to arrive as a
# transitive peer, and its disappearance broke every test file at once.
legacy-peer-deps=true
